ROCR-Runtime (pinned tag rocm-6.4.4): stop immediate GPU virtual-address
(VA) recycling in libhsakmt's fmm aperture allocator, which corrupts MIOpen
CK reduction kernels on gfx803 (Polaris), by deferring freed-VAs on a
length-bounded FIFO; and default the hsa-runtime SimpleHeap fragment
sub-allocator to OFF, since it is a second, higher-layer VA recycler that
the fmm fix cannot see.

WHY
---
miopenReduceTensor (dynamic/CompositeKernel reduction path) on gfx803
intermittently returns wrong sums for a subset of output columns --
surfaced as ONNX Runtime ReductionOpTest.ReduceSum_apex_matrix_large and
reproduced by a standalone 52-shape MIOpen-only harness
(gfx803/tools/reduce-harness/harness_exp.cpp). Root cause, pinned by
controlled harness modes (HAR_MODE=reuse vs fresh-distinct addresses):

  * recycled input/output device addresses => ~10/52 shapes wrong
  * never-recycled (fresh-distinct) addresses => 0/52 wrong

The VA recycling happens at TWO layers, and both must be blocked:

  1. libhsa SimpleHeap fragment sub-allocator
     (runtime/hsa-runtime/core/util/simple_heap.h, used from
     core/driver/kfd/amd_kfd_driver.cpp): caches freed 2MB blocks and
     re-serves the SAME VA for the next sub-2MB allocation, entirely
     above libhsakmt -- an fmm-level fix alone cannot observe these.
     Empirical: with only the fmm defer active and this allocator
     re-enabled, the harness still fails 10/52 (3/3 runs).

  2. libhsakmt fmm aperture free-list (libhsakmt/src/fmm.c,
     __fmm_release -> aperture_release_area): a freed VA goes straight
     back onto the free-list and the next same-size allocation gets the
     same address. With layer 1 disabled but layer 2 stock, the harness
     still fails ~6/52.

Why recycled VAs mis-read on gfx803 specifically is still open (stale
L2/TLB/GART state on re-map; Polaris-only, does not reproduce on newer
architectures). A MIOpen-level ring of K fresh input buffers was proven
to fix it (0/52 at K>=16), which is exactly the behaviour this patch
moves down into the runtime so ALL consumers (MIOpen, ORT, PyTorch)
get it without per-framework patches.

Two non-solutions, measured and rejected:

  * HSA_DISABLE_FRAGMENT_ALLOCATOR=1 alone (stock lib): harness 10 -> 6,
    ORT still 1 reduce-fail. Necessary but not sufficient.
  * Never returning freed VAs at all ("vanoreuse" leak): harness clean
    but VA space exhausts under real workloads -- ORT ReductionOpTest
    went from 28.5s to a 900s timeout with 400+ failures. Any fix must
    return VAs to the free-list, just not immediately.

WHAT
----
Two changes:

  1. libhsakmt/src/fmm.c -- deferred release. __fmm_release() still frees
     the KFD buffer object and physical pages IMMEDIATELY (no memory
     footprint change), but the VA stays reserved: the vm_object is kept
     in the aperture tree and parked on a per-aperture FIFO
     (deferred_head/deferred_tail/deferred_len). Only when the FIFO
     exceeds FMM_REUSE_QUEUE_MAX (64) entries is the oldest entry's area
     actually returned to the free-list -- so a freed address cannot be
     re-handled to a new allocation until at least 64 intervening frees,
     comfortably exceeding the proven ring-16 freshness window, while the
     queue stays length-bounded and O(1) per op (nothing leaks; VA space
     usage is bounded by 64 reserved areas per aperture).

     Scratch-backing apertures bypass the FIFO: they are torn down only
     at process exit by fmm_release_scratch(), whose loop requires each
     released object to leave the tree immediately -- the first version of
     this patch without the bypass deadlocked (well, livelocked: 100% user
     CPU in that loop) in hsa_shut_down() -> ~GpuAgent -> hsakmt_fmm_release
     -> fmm_release_scratch. Marker: GFX803_VA_REUSE_DEFER_PATCH.

     Deferred objects are hardened against stale-handle replay: their
     handles[] are scrubbed to 0 and object->deferred is set when the
     release is parked, and __fmm_release() no-ops on an already-deferred
     object. Without this, a second hsakmt_fmm_release() of the same
     address (the object is still findable in the tree, unlike stock
     where it is gone) would re-issue FREE_MEMORY_OF_GPU on handle ids
     KFD may already have recycled to a different, live BO -- observed as
     intermittent mid-run GPU page faults ("Page not present") in the ORT
     suite at ~3/40 runs with the unhardened patch, 0/10 after hardening.

  2. runtime/hsa-runtime/core/util/flag.h -- invert the
     HSA_DISABLE_FRAGMENT_ALLOCATOR default: the SimpleHeap fragment
     sub-allocator (layer 1 above) is now OFF unless the variable is
     explicitly set to "0". Measured cost on the real gates: none
     (ORT ReductionOpTest 28.4s vs 28.5s stock; it would only show up in
     alloc/free-hot microbenchmarks). Keeping layer 1 enabled is NOT an
     option: with it on, the harness fails 10/52 even with the fmm defer
     active (3/3 runs).

GATES (all run on the gfx803 box, ROCm 6.4.4, against this patched lib)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
  * harness_exp, HAR_MODE=reuse, 52-shape sweep, 5 consecutive runs:
    0/52 failed every run, ~0.7s wall each (baseline stock: 6-10/52).
  * ORT onnxruntime_test_all --gtest_filter=*ReductionOpTest* (317 tests),
    3 consecutive runs: 315 OK / 0 FAILED / 2 pre-existing SKIPs,
    ~28.5s wall each -- identical wall time to stock, and the
    ReduceSum_apex_matrix_large failure is gone.
  * Process-exit is clean (no teardown spin; see the scratch-aperture
    bypass above).

